  "query_preview": "..High pressure will remain across the region through \nthe first half of the week. Temperatures will continue to be very \nwarm with highs around 10-20 degrees above normal through Tuesday. \nSome slightly cooler air arrives around midweek but temperatures \nwill still remain around 5-10 degrees above normal through the end \nof the week. \n\nAs we head towards mid week, the ridge will shift eastward and a \nshortwave moving along the outside of the ridge. This wave will stay \nmostly to our west before crossing the area on Wednesday. Winds will \nturn more to the south-southwest, which will allow for decent \nmoisture advection starting on Monday. Most areas will remain dry \nthrough midweek but some isolated showers or virga are possible, \nespecially near the White Mountains on Monday and Tuesday. By \nWednesday, we should have enough moisture around for additional \nshowers to develop across the higher terrain, with some isolated \nthunderstorms, too. QPF amounts continue to look very light. \n\nThursday and beyond...behind the wave the flow looks to become more \nzonal, though some models show a trough dipping south along the \nGreat Basin. For now it looks like temperatures will cool of \nslightly, but remain above normal through the end of the week with \ndry conditions returning. Expect southwest breezes typical for \nthis time of year during each afternoon.",
